platformio source package
=========================

platformio is third-party upstream (https://pypi.org/project/platformio/);
only debian/ is ours. There is no copy of the source in this repository and
no orig tarball on disk: the sdist is fetched from PyPI at build time by
.forgejo/registry-upload, which resolves its URL through the PyPI API and
checks it against debian/upstream/sdist.sha256 before unpacking it.

A version bump is two edits:

 * debian/changelog -- a new entry, version <ver>-1~ppa<N>, UNRELEASED.
 * debian/upstream/sdist.sha256 -- the new sdist's name and sha256, as
   "sha256sum platformio-<ver>.tar.gz" would write them.

The script derives the upstream version from the changelog, so those two
have to agree; a mismatch stops the build before the download.

The build is not dpkg-buildpackage in place. The script copies debian/ into
the unpacked sdist, builds a source package with dpkg-source -b, unpacks
that again and builds the binary from the unpacked copy. That extra step is
what applies debian/patches: dpkg-buildpackage -b in the sdist would skip
the series entirely and silently produce a package with upstream's own pins.

One debian/ serves noble, resolute and stonking. The committed changelog
entry carries the packaging version alone and whatever builds it appends the
series. The package is Architecture: all, but one build still cannot serve
all three: dh_python3 writes ${python3:Depends} from the packages it finds
installed, and the three series carry rather different versions of bottle,
click, starlette and tabulate.

Every pip dependency is in Ubuntu for all three series except ajsonrpc,
which entered the archive in stonking; noble and resolute take it from the
Forgejo registry's python-ajsonrpc backport, which registry-upload adds to
the container's apt sources before resolving build dependencies. Build it
first if both are new.

Tests are disabled in the build (PYBUILD_DISABLE=test). The sdist carries
one test module, tests/test_examples.py, and it clones example projects and
downloads toolchains; debian/tests/control smoke-tests the installed
package instead.

PlatformIO downloads toolchains, platform definitions and PIO Home into
~/.platformio at runtime, per project. Its "pio upgrade" and pip self-update
paths hit PEP 668 on a system install, which is expected and left alone.

